 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> The flight is in fingertip strong left. Where are you as #4?</summary><p>On the left, outboard of #3 on #3’s bearing line. The second element is on the strong side; #2 is alone on the right.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> In a diamond, what references does #4 use?</summary><p>Close-trail references to get into the slot behind lead, then the fingertip bearing lines of #2 and #3 to make the diamond symmetrical.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Lead wants echelon right from fingertip strong right. Who moves, and in what order?</summary><p>Lead signals #3 first to move out and make room, then #2, who crosses under to the right side. Signalling #2 first is a common error.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> During an element cross-under, where does #4 go?</summary><p>With #3, crossing #3’s 6 o’clock and continuing to the outboard position, so it keeps every aircraft in sight.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> How does fluid four differ from division wall?</summary><p>In both, −1 and −3 are in combat spread. In wall the wingmen are also in spread, line abreast; in fluid four they fly tac wing, 0.2–0.6 nm from their leads on the cruise bearing line or aft.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Division wall, order −2, −1, −3, −4 from left to right. Lead calls a tac turn left. Who turns first?</summary><p>−4, on the outside of the turn. −3 turns as −4’s intakes come round toward it, then −1, then −2.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Fluid four makes an in-place turn. You are the wingman on the inside. Where do you roll out?</summary><p>On the outside, still in tac wing, looking through your section lead to regain sight of the other section.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> You are −3 of a three-ship in cloud and you lose sight of lead. Lead calls a fan breakup. What do you do?</summary><p>Call “three has lost sight”, continue your lost sight turn to 60° away from the base heading and climb to the base altitude; after 30 seconds return to the base heading. Lead climbs 1,000 ft above base and −2 500 ft above, so the three of you are separated. Squawk and follow lead’s join-up or IFR instructions.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Under UK military rules, a jet close formation plans an aerobatic display. Cloud base is 1,200 ft. Can it go?</summary><p>Not with aerobatics: jet and turboprop formations need a 1,500 ft cloud base for aerobatic display flying. A flypast or non-aerobatic display needs 1,000 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> How close may a fast jet formation run in toward the crowd?</summary><p>No closer than 230 m laterally, increased to 450 m when above 300 KIAS and heading toward spectators.</p></details>

